WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.510
 Delicious Revolution is brought to you by us, by me and Chelsea, specifically by our consulting business.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:11.330
 We provide participatory research, communications, and creative work to organizations that are building food movements.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.190
 What's your food movement? Let us know and get in touch. deliciousrevolutionshow.com

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:28.350
 In the Delta and in this county, the outcomes for kids in particular are horrendous.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:33.670
 Like, you know, the obesity outcomes for kids under five, I mean, it's really, really bad. Like, it's endemic, right?

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:38.800
 So, then I start to think, there's this gap, right? There's this huge gap.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.340
 I'm talking to people that are in their 80s and 90s that are telling me, "Do you know how to make a mincemeat pie?"

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.920
 Okay, well, you have to pick the green tomatoes and then you dry them on the roof, right?

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.920
 You know, I'm going, "Oh, I always thought it was raisins. No, it's tomatoes." You know, whatever.

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:56.520
 I'm having this conversation with people about this sort of deep, deep history of how you make these recipes that I love.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:02.800
 And then I talk to people that are a generation below, "I haven't made a pie crust in 30 years."

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.520
 Or, "Oh, just go get you one at the store. Go get a Pillsbury one that has all this sh*t in it," you know?

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.070
 So, there's something, something happened. And you can look systemically at what happened.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:18.390
 You can look at why this happened and why this was strategic in many ways, but it still exists.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:24.870
 This information still exists. Live and in living color for another maybe 10 years, this information exists, right?

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:31.590
 So, I don't know. I don't know what the vehicle is yet, right? And that's what I hope to explore.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:38.310
 But I know that it's this precious moment where you have people that remember how to grow and cook

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:45.030
 and preserve food that are alive at the same time as people who have never shopped outside of, like, the Quick Stop.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:48.880
 There's a huge opportunity there, so how do you connect that?

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:55.110
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:56.550
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:08.550
 And I'm Devin Sampson. We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of food, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists, bringing you in-depth conversations with some amazing people.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:25.440
 On this third season of Delicious Revolution, we're bringing you stories and perspectives from the unseen places in food systems, going behind kitchen doors, into underground nests of native bees, under the waves, and to the far away origins of flavors we love, just to name a few.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:33.490
 I'm speaking with people who work with food in places we normally cannot see or don't notice. It's a season of unseen stories of food.

