 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes or your favorite podcast app.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.870
 Just search for Delicious Revolution wherever you get your podcasts, and you'll find us.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:49.360
 Our website is deliciousrevolutionshow.com, where we have pictures, links, and more information about all the people on our show.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:55.070
 Michaela Leslie Rule is a digital media producer, storyteller, and social scientist.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.410
 Michaela is the owner of Fact Memory Testimony.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:05.210
 Embedded in Michaela's approach to research, advocacy, and communication is elevating constituent voices through the use of storytelling.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:14.370
 She's particularly interested in participatory methods for measuring and documenting social and organizational change, and she has designed and implemented participatory projects on four continents.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.810
 She uses a story-centric approach to produce multimedia projects and advocacy campaigns.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:22.930
 Here's Chelsea's interview with Michaela Leslie Rule.

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:25.750
 Hi, Michael, and welcome to the show.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:26.590
 Thank you.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:36.650
 So you do a lot of work around food traditions, and your work is also focused in you came to food traditions from public health.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.050
 It's one of your sets of interests.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.530
 Can you explain that?

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:40.790
 Can you tell that story?

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:41.450
 Yeah.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:04:12.610
 Before I did public health, I came from a performing arts background, so I was really interested in stories, and I went to public health school and studied global health and the impact of stories on global health specifically, because I've always been, I guess, preoccupied in a way with how you see policy created or how you see it enforced and how infrequently you see people that are impacted by that policy actually sitting at the table or helping to design that policy.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:24.670
 So, yeah, so I went to public health school, and it was great in many ways, and I think it teaches, it taught me sort of a framework for understanding health more broadly.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:42.030
 It was, the school that I went to was interested in community development and was interested in, I think, in a sort of, from a messaging perspective, was interested in diversity of opinions, but I think the reality of the situation is that it was, you know, I went to a school that was primarily,

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:54.940
 run by and taught by professors of medicine who were practicing physicians, and they have their own sort of biases towards how much information they know and the academy knows versus people that are living within the communities.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:17.550
 So, yeah, so, yeah, so doing work there and learning a little bit about indigenous knowledge there through IRI, which is, I went to University of Washington, and so there's a group there that's the Indian Wellness Research Institute, I think, and they are specifically interested in the idea of sort of both historical trauma and then indigenous knowledge and how that's passed down.

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:42.380
 And I became, I think, I think, and I became, I think, then interested in places, you know, globally that sort of transfer of knowledge and looked at that through gender-based violence and looking at stories that women tell their children and especially their sons and sort of how do you transfer information that informs cultural norms or informs how people behave, basically.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:57.890
 And sort of leap forward five years and doing, you know, work on a variety of issues, I decided to come back to the States after several years living abroad to focus specifically on communities that I had a direct investment in.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:11.130
 And for me, that was the Mississippi Delta, where my mom was born, where my grandmother was born, sort of six or seven generations of my family, that I had, we, I just, my mom had broken ties in many ways with the Deep South for a variety of reasons.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:19.690
 And I, three or four years ago, began going back and talking to my, what we talk about as kinfolk.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:23.250
 I have no idea really how I'm related to people, but I am related, and it's a small community.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:32.050
 And in those conversations, in order to get access to information about the history of my family and about the history of the town, I often spoke about food.

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:36.430
 We would often sit down and start to talk about food, or we would talk about culture over food.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:56.510
 And I was struck by how my public health education had really prepared me for this, this kind of, to approach the South and specifically the Southeast and rural communities from a position of deficit, that this was, they were lacking something, that they were, you know, they needed something, basically, that this knowledge didn't exist.

00:06:56.630 --> 00:07:00.790
 And through these conversations, I realized that it does exist, that it's, that it's there.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:07.460
 And there's a question about transference, and there's also a question about, I hate the word empowered.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:18.360
 I think it's, it's something about valuing, something about how do you work with communities that have this incredible information, both agricultural and culinary information.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:30.230
 How do you work within those communities and create tools to transfer information that really value, value the fact that this is not an outside intervention, that everything they need, all of the tools are actually in their community.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:34.630
 So I think, in a windy way, that's, is that good?

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:35.590
 Yeah, that's great.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:36.890
 That makes a lot of sense.

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:39.010
 Let's back up a little bit.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:39.190
 Great.

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:46.380
 So, I don't really understand what public health does in a lot of ways.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:53.610
 When I think of, like, progressive public health projects, I think of, like, those stoves or whatever.

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:54.350
 Oh, good.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:54.930
 Yeah.

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:56.330
 Limiting smoke inside.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:56.790
 Right.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:57.410
 Smokeless stoves.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:57.630
 Right.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:58.430
 Smokeless stoves.

00:07:58.550 --> 00:08:02.630
 So that would be, like, so people would not have lung disease or something in their kitchen.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:11.230
 And when I think of not very forward-thinking public health projects, I think of, like, or maybe more standard.

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:11.710
 I don't know.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.570
 I'm not very forward.

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:20.840
 I think of, like, lots of, you know, I think of water and, like, water.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:22.000
 Sanitation.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:22.840
 Sanitation.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:44.520
 And I think of kind of these systemic things that I think are very important, but oftentimes for me, like, feel really intricately linked with the state and feel really top-down and feel like a lot of times they're kind of a one-size-fits-all sort of approach to how to keep people from dying.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:47.720
 I mean, it's not even necessarily about quality of life.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:49.400
 Lots and lots of times.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.820
 Well, I think it's – I mean, I think I know what you're saying.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:56.120
 I think – so when I think about public health, I think broadly.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:57.620
 I think expansively.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:05.560
 I think of – when I think about food and nutrition, I think about it as a systemic issue, but I also think about it as being very individual to the community that you're in.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:10.720
 And that's what I think is interesting about the idea of public health is that you can have a very systemic conversation.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:15.980
 You can talk about, you know, the impact of Monsanto, you know, on communities of color.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:22.700
 You can talk about them – you know, specifically, you can talk about lobbying and how that impacts sort of food that's available in a state or, you know, in a community.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:30.860
 And at the same time, you can also talk about – so on a behavioral level, when you're talking about individual and family behavior, that is not a system-wide intervention.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:36.140
 I mean, you can do messaging at the state level about, you know, working on diabetes or whatever, right?

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:55.660
 But if you're not talking about people's individual – not individual, but family cultures, community cultures, you're not talking about honoring the fact that people have an entire food tradition that's based in not only history but memory and, like, you know, in sort of a sense of self, like a deep sense of your identity and yourself is around food.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:10:00.520
 Like, for everybody, even if you don't – even if you're vegan and you don't – you know, I just said if you're vegan and you don't eat.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:14.440
 But basically, you know, even if you come from a tradition where it's about, you know, either a tradition of extreme affluence where it's about being very choosy about what you eat or a position of extreme sort of deprivation and poverty where you don't have any choices,

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:18.320
 people always have this very deep embedded sense of food.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:29.440
 So when we talk about – I think you're right to say – I think a lot of energy and time is spent on doing this sort of one message meets every – you know, the sort of painting with a broad brush.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:35.550
 Saying, you know, we're going to spend millions of dollars on creating one campaign that's going to serve all of these people.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:42.110
 But I think that's part of what I think find really fascinating is that we know that that doesn't work, you know, because people come from different places.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:45.750
 We're all sort of coming from different perspectives and looking at this message in very different ways.

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:51.160
 And for some people, some people, it's life or death about giving up your culture.

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:54.720
 So these two things are held equally.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:04.760
 Like, you can die from diabetes, but what does it mean to sit at a table with your great-grandchildren and not be able to eat the food that you've eaten for your entire life as part of this celebration, right?

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:17.810
 I think what's interesting to me is what – if we recognize that, that there's something important about the tradition itself, about sitting – about cooking those things with – like my great-aunt cooks with her great-great-grandchildren.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:19.530
 She cooks with them.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:20.290
 They come over.

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:24.380
 They're frying food, and she sits on a chair, and she tells them what they need to do, right?

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:27.940
 For her not to participate in eating that food would be heartbreaking.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:30.920
 It's like, you know, so how do you navigate that?

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:33.880
 You know, how do you talk to people about that?

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:36.600
 Which is, to me, is also about public health.

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:40.840
 Well, so that was my – thank you for making that a little bit more articulate.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:50.280
 I was trying to describe my sort of clunky knowledge of public health because when I think about storytelling, I think about specifics, right?

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:58.480
 And I think about these really – I think about culture, but I especially think about very individual experiences of life and how sometimes they exist in these bigger cultures.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.480
 And sometimes they exist in these really –

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:00.800
 Family.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:01.860
 Right, right.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:04.420
 Very small or even individual ways, right?

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:09.040
 How do those two things meet in your work?

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:15.180
 You know, I think, you know, I – my happy place is working on stories.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.140
 I like to sit down on someone's couch or at their kitchen table.

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:31.520
 And specifically in the Delta with this work, you know, I've been really lucky to get – I think partly because of family name, I get some – there's some familiarity and some acceptance about this person that has, you know, on face value.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:32.900
 Like, I have no connection with the South.

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:34.120
 You know, I'm a Northerner.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.200
 I'm, like, immediately recognizable as not being from the community.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:39.540
 And I've been given this tremendous access.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:46.090
 For me, it's the – I think you said early – oh, sorry.

00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:47.350
 I think the top-down idea.

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:50.690
 But I think it really is this thing that emerges from the bottom, right?

00:12:50.730 --> 00:13:01.160
 You start with a story, and the story – for me, the story at the kitchen table starts to sort of get me thinking about these other – either other strands or other things that I've heard about.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.280
 And eager to sort of relate.

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:06.850
 It's like to validate in some way, right?

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:12.050
 I want to find some triangulation or some way of validating, like, how does this story fit into this larger narrative?

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:14.190
 And if it doesn't, why is that?

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:15.610
 So where is this narrative coming from?

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:24.640
 So for me, it's a way of becoming critical of this other broader narrative about who these people are.

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:38.820
 Specific – I mean, for me, as a Black woman, this sort of desire to not be caught in believing these statistics about who I am or who I'm going to become as an elderly Black woman, you know, when I grow up, grow further.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:43.180
 For me, there's something that is deeply personal to me about that.

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.420
 And so, I mean, you could say public health, right?

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.400
 But for me, in this instance, it's private health.

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:52.860
 It's about, you know, it's about these stories are informing who I want to become, right?

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:59.300
 And what beliefs I hold about this community that is very deeply a part of my past and of my family.

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:19.250
 Yeah, so I think something to think about with this is that you were talking about this idea of how health is oftentimes seen as life and death in these big ways, right?

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:21.850
 In these structural ways, in these systemic ways.

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:34.890
 So, eat more vegetables, drink clean water, and then it's not seen as life or death when your aunt can't participate, your great aunt can't participate in this cooking tradition or whatever.

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:42.520
 But both really do have these dire consequences and also these incredible rewards, right?

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:53.640
 So, it feels good to drink enough water, but it also feels essential to your being to cook with your great-grande, races and nephews, right?

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:59.200
 So, how do they, like, in this work for you, is there this untangling or this comfort?

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:02.610
 I don't find those things to be at odds with one another.

00:15:02.770 --> 00:15:05.990
 And I think that that is part of this, that's part of the critique in some ways.

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:09.090
 That's part of, like, really taking the time to listen to people.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:14.770
 And I think an example of that, like, the fruits and vegetables idea is sort of this idea that you're going to eat salad.

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:18.370
 Like, often the example is a cold salad.

00:15:19.310 --> 00:15:24.030
 We're, like, the West is really, that's the only place where people eat cold food.

00:15:24.230 --> 00:15:26.710
 Like, you know, or eat cold vegetables in particular.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:30.570
 So, you know, that's, you know, you travel all over the world.

00:15:30.650 --> 00:15:31.270
 You live all over the world.

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:32.930
 And people have disdain for it.

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:33.830
 It's like it's not healthy.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:34.570
 It's not good for you.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:40.730
 There are entire traditions around health and to the Chinese, you know, Chinese traditions about not eating cold food, you know?

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:48.310
 So, this example is, this example is that if you don't eat, the image is of this salad bowl and that this is what it means to be healthy.

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:54.690
 But if you talk to people, you realize that people eat collard greens, they're, like, making cabbage, they have, you know, green beans.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:55.210
 All right.

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:58.010
 We just got interrupted for a second by a trash show.

00:15:58.250 --> 00:16:04.970
 So, we were talking about how these two, you know, the different ideas of health are not antithetical to each other.

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:05.690
 Yeah.

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:06.770
 And then it's about, yeah.

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:13.420
 So, I was just going to say that in the public health model, often when we talk about food nutrition, it's very restrictive.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:18.780
 It's very, like, it's in there providing very limited examples of what behavioral change might look like.

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:22.180
 And it's kind of, they're severe examples.

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:29.280
 And so, this idea of eating vegetables as raw vegetables, you know, carrot sticks and things, that's not the only option.

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:31.660
 And there's actually no reason for them actually to be selling that option.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:39.970
 It seems to be, that's more, in my mind, I think that's more about placing excessive value on one particular way of thinking about eating,

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:45.160
 which is really a white, affluent, Western way of eating.

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:49.760
 And there are so many other ways to think about eating healthy vegetables, eating vegetables every day.

00:16:49.840 --> 00:16:55.680
 But that's not really, that's not part of the broader sort of message, especially in the United States, that we're sending out, right?

00:16:55.680 --> 00:17:00.360
 We're saying if you don't eat like people that live in Boston and have access to whole foods, then you're not being healthy.

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:01.780
 And that doesn't work.

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:06.200
 It doesn't work specifically in this community in the South.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:18.190
 What does work and what is interesting is when you have nutritionists that are from the community that are talking in real terms about the recipes that they know that they themselves grew up with,

00:17:18.690 --> 00:17:22.270
 and that the household that they're visiting grew up in.

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:27.930
 And you talk about whether or not you need to have salted pork in the cabbage, for example.

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:30.890
 Well, you know, and what would you substitute that with?

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:33.510
 Not just eat the pork with no seasonings.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:35.250
 Like what would the seasonings actually look like?

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:43.230
 And often you find that the oldest person in the house actually knows how to prepare the food in another way because they know how to grow the food.

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:52.350
 Because they were doing that on, you know, as slaves, as sharecroppers on, you know, they have private small family gardens.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:56.450
 Most people have a garden, you know, but not the way we have gardens in California.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:17:59.950
 Like they have expansive gardens because people have acres of land around them.

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:02.270
 People are growing their own vegetables.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:06.030
 And that knowledge comes from this older generation, including preparation.

00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:14.900
 Like what do you do in times of scarcity, for example, is, you know, would be an interesting starting point when you talk about what needs to be in a recipe and what doesn't.

00:18:14.900 --> 00:18:17.450
 We were talking about resiliency.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:24.540
 This idea that traditions are static is, I think, also part of that myth that you're unpacking, right?

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:27.640
 So people have been making these recipes for a long time.

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:28.780
 People know how to feed themselves.

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:35.390
 People know how to feed themselves the right food for who they are and where they live and what's available.

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:38.690
 It's not like, in some ways, I think this is not rocket science.

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:39.930
 It just has to be introduced.

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:40.560
 Yeah.

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:45.520
 But so do you only make one, do you only make a recipe one way?

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:59.160
 Like, well, that's, I mean, I think that's, that's part of what makes, I mean, when you, when you, when you ask, when you, when you ask someone how to make like chicken livers and rice or something, right?

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:02.120
 You ask someone, everybody has their own way of doing this, right?

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:09.930
 When you're in small communities, especially communities where, you know, the woman on the corner was the teacher for so-and-so who, you know, there's this sort of this.

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.840
 There are these dynamics basically that evolve over time.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:15.800
 These, these sort of cultural, cultural community ties.

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:24.260
 People are really honest about who makes the best thing, you know, who, who makes the best thing and why they think it's the best thing.

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:28.460
 Or they'll say, well, if you like peach cobbler with crust on the bottom, this is who you need to talk to.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:36.620
 But if you like it, the other, you know, there's, there's some, it's, there's an appreciation for how recipes change and how they're unique to different people.

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:37.060
 Right.

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:40.620
 So I think, I mean, that's one thing, yeah, recipes are not static.

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:52.340
 But the other thing is that this assumption that it's a, it's a, a linear transmission of information, that it just goes from one generation to the next, to the next, to the next, as this unchanged thing is totally ridiculous.

00:19:52.340 --> 00:20:01.710
 That's one, and it gets to this idea of authenticity, which I think people talk about in communities of color from the States in ways that they don't with other, with sort of white mainstream communities.

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:05.910
 And I find that this idea that we're static, that, you know, just came directly through slavery.

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:17.780
 You know, it's just this, it's this horrible, antiquated way of talking about people, which is one of the reasons that I think when you look at a public health conversation, you get into this, this is the only way to cook the food and it's wrong.

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:29.520
 And there's no space to have a conversation about, you know, the diversity of ways in which it's prepared or how it's prepared in different regions in the South or how it's prepared in the North for that matter, like post great, great migration.

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:36.240
 Like what came with people and how did the food change when they came to Chicago or Detroit or Oakland or whatever, right?

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:42.660
 So I think there's a more looking at that sort of broader diaspora of sort of African-American food and food traditions.

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:57.070
 I think within that are so many different ways of preparing food and so many different places to draw on for knowledge as we, as we want to and try to address these major community level and also systemic, right?

00:20:57.290 --> 00:21:11.440
 Issues, but specifically looking at behavioral issues or something about drawing from within rather than, you know, rather than talking about what is within as being something that is deficient in some way.

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:19.840
 And so therefore we have to go outside, you know, and I think also appreciating the fact that we're already outside, like we're all already outside, right?

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:27.520
 Like we're all interacting with food in many different ways in different cities and different, you know, locations with various levels of access.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:29.600
 And that also changes the way food is prepared.

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:32.140
 Yeah, we live in a globalized world, right?

00:21:32.260 --> 00:21:34.440
 And nobody is exempt from that.

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:34.680
 Right.

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:37.740
 So that is just how it goes.

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:39.720
 So things change over time, right?

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:47.030
 And so what, let's go back to this nutritionist idea, like these in town nutritionists or whatever.

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:49.320
 Tell me what they do.

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:51.820
 Tell me what it's like for them to go into people's houses.

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:56.470
 And like, what are the issues that people are talking about and trying to figure out?

00:21:56.930 --> 00:22:03.590
 So my, and this is in some ways is limited experience in the South particular, but again, I'll also speak more broadly about what I know.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:19.390
 Community health workers are often, you know, sort of low, not nutritionist, basically, but sort of lower skilled or like lower, lesser trained, I guess, academically trained people that are from the community that are recognizable as part of the community.

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:32.440
 And they are, so hypertension, for example, looking at sort of limited salt intake is really hard for people that are often already in their 60s or 70s, right?

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:40.340
 Because this is now an entire lifetime of, of sort of, you know, one, one experience of food and expectations around food.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:46.380
 Um, and I can speak directly about my, my great uncle, he's 90 and he's, he's phenomenal.

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:47.420
 He's like amazing.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:51.640
 Um, and he taught me to like smoke cigars and drink white lightning, which is moonshine.

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:54.460
 And like, we go to the South and we, you know, we hang out and everything.

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:56.560
 And it's always sort of, you know, it's been that way.

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:06.000
 Um, and then a few years ago, he had, he's had heart issues in the past, but he had heart issues, um, about a year ago, a year and a half ago, something like that.

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:11.080
 And ended up in the hospital and the doctor, the doctors said no more salt, right?

00:23:11.140 --> 00:23:19.680
 Not how much, so no more salt, a hundred percent is not, if someone gave me that, you know, cause I'm, I'm younger and I have a totally different relationship to doctors.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:25.280
 So my response would be, I want to actually know, like, I want to know how many milligrams we're talking about.

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:29.620
 You know, I want to know because I know it's not reasonable to say you're not eating any salt at all.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:29.960
 Right.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:33.740
 What they mean is no added salt, but added salt to what, right?

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:38.360
 Like to, to, are we talking about added salt to like you, you salting your, your food at the table?

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.020
 Are we talking about specific kinds of food?

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:47.360
 Like eating barbecue probably isn't, you know, the best idea, but barbecue dry or barbecue with sauce and like who made the sauce?

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:53.880
 You know, are we talking about store sauce or sauce that you made, you know, you're making with tomatoes and maple and, you know, over the stove.

00:23:54.020 --> 00:24:00.630
 So the idea is, it's like the instructions that he received were one is that they're a form of shorthand.

00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:12.250
 I think it's incredibly disrespectful to give people, especially older people, and then expect them then to translate to this entire history, this entire cultural tradition, basically single handedly.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:13.150
 That doesn't really make sense.

00:24:13.250 --> 00:24:20.480
 Community health workers take this information that comes from physicians and they help to apply it to the foods that you eat every day.

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:28.950
 So making distinctions, like, are we talking about the barbecue that my cousin makes behind the house that we have made for several years?

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:35.870
 Or are we talking about barbecue sauce that's sold in the store that has corn syrup and all of this, you know, added salt in it, right?

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:44.730
 It's about making distinctions and not being so dogmatic, like, about what is and is not allowed.

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:53.180
 So I think when I think about the community health workers, I think about them sort of having an appreciation for what exists, for the value that exists there.

00:24:53.680 --> 00:24:59.090
 And also being a little bit more flexible to what is actually possible, like within what amount of time.

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:02.390
 Like you don't leave the hospital with a diagnosis and then just suddenly change your whole life.

00:25:02.470 --> 00:25:03.150
 That doesn't happen.

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:04.190
 It doesn't happen for anybody.

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:17.400
 So, you know, what does that change look like and sort of how does it happen and where and also where can you acknowledge people's progress, you know, within sort of dietary restrictions in this case?

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:29.430
 You know, where do you acknowledge that and how do you congratulate people, you know, on being able to make progress around their own health in the ways that work for them, right?

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:36.980
 And I also think, you know, you spoke earlier, we were talking earlier about life or death situations.

00:25:36.980 --> 00:25:40.320
 And I think there's also a really big piece of that that's about how do you want to live.

00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:42.140
 And we don't talk about that.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:48.200
 I think there's this massive assumption that everyone wants to live to 150 and, you know, and I'll do anything I can to get there.

00:25:48.260 --> 00:26:01.050
 But we sort of shy away from both when we talk about physical health and mental health and, you know, and when we talk about nutrition, we don't I don't think we talk enough about how do you want to exist in the world?

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:05.470
 Like, how do you want to engage with your family and with other people?

00:26:05.470 --> 00:26:10.070
 And I think for older people in particular, that's something that deserves at least a question.

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:18.910
 You know, I think, you know, and I'm not I'm not advocating for, you know, just letting people rot because they don't want to like I'm not I'm not advocating for that.

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:25.610
 But I think there's something really important going back to this idea of what it means to be sitting on the on the stool watching your great granddaughter fry fish.

00:26:26.590 --> 00:26:32.210
 And I think there's something to be said for what that what that what joy that brings, you know.

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:34.110
 Absolutely.

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:40.630
 And I think that quality of resilience is really underrated within that, too.

00:26:40.930 --> 00:26:41.150
 Right.

00:26:41.150 --> 00:26:54.190
 So I think everything you say sort of just really complexifies the story of what public health is in somewhere like, you know, in the Mississippi Delta in a small town would look like.

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:54.490
 Right.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:56.970
 Like, how have people made it for a long time?

00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:58.790
 What are the things they've done to do that?

00:26:58.890 --> 00:27:00.510
 What are those behavioral choices?

00:27:01.030 --> 00:27:04.480
 This exists in this complex web of things.

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:04.760
 Right.

00:27:04.820 --> 00:27:10.170
 Of access and of tradition and and of all these knowledge bases.

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:10.530
 Right.

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:15.940
 So I love this idea of like salt of taking that out.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:17.400
 It's because it seems like such a simple thing.

00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:17.600
 Right.

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:19.400
 It's like don't put salt on your food or whatever.

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:28.140
 But I think about a friend who after he's in his mid 80s and the doctor told him, you can't eat tortillas.

00:27:28.340 --> 00:27:32.900
 You can only eat brown bimbo bread from the store.

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:33.380
 Right.

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:37.000
 Which is like the air puffed bread that you get in rural Mexico.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:39.020
 And he just said, I can never get full.

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:42.720
 The problem here is that there's like no nutrition in my food.

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:44.920
 And his wife doesn't know how to cook it.

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:48.620
 And so she's fighting him about it every single day.

00:27:49.060 --> 00:27:53.780
 And, you know, she doesn't know how to make the right amount of tortillas now because he's not eating tortillas.

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:57.000
 It throws off this whole bigger ecosystem.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:01.980
 The problem is exactly of how this, how this family works.

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:04.140
 And I mean, what's balance?

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:06.340
 Like where do those stories change?

00:28:06.340 --> 00:28:09.760
 And like what, like what's health in a story like that?

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:11.040
 What's your uncle decide to do?

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:12.200
 How do you figure it out?

00:28:12.260 --> 00:28:15.320
 My uncle has decided that he wants to, he just turned 90.

00:28:15.420 --> 00:28:18.180
 We went to, we had a big party for him about a month ago.

00:28:19.180 --> 00:28:22.420
 He has decided that he is going to live to a hundred.

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:24.060
 He's like, this is what I'm doing.

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:31.700
 And he said, you know, he, he has a new love of his life now that he, you know, they, they're lovely and they travel around the country together.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:35.120
 And he's basically decided, he's decided what's important to him.

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:43.810
 And for him, he misses his white lightning and he misses, you know, his barbecue and he misses, you know, these, these things.

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:52.680
 But for him, the, we talked about like this sort of this, these, these family connections and what that looks like and how it feels and how, what you connect over.

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:53.270
 Right.

00:28:54.030 --> 00:28:58.600
 And for him, his connection with family has sort of transformed, right?

00:28:58.600 --> 00:29:03.940
 Like he's making all of these trips, you know, he's flying to, he came to see my house when I bought the house.

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:05.820
 He's like, I'll be on a plane as soon as you get a house.

00:29:05.840 --> 00:29:07.080
 And 10 days later, he was here.

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:09.500
 I had no furniture, you know, it was just, you know, he was here.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:16.740
 And we, we collectively, my mother and my aunt, you know, we're all learning how to, you know, adapt to sort of his food restrictions.

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:30.010
 And at the same time, in some ways, I feel really grateful that I have the background I do, because when he says, oh, I'm, I eat Thousand Island dressing from so-and-so store, then I'm like, oh, okay, well, and that doesn't affect him poorly.

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:34.430
 You go, oh, okay, well, the doctor's obviously not saying no salt at all.

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:40.450
 And you weren't having some massive, you know, you know, sort of coronary episode because you're having this tiny amount of dressing.

00:29:40.670 --> 00:29:46.390
 So where, how can we, how can we sort of think about that in terms of the amount of salt that you're having during the day?

00:29:46.490 --> 00:29:46.650
 Right.

00:29:46.670 --> 00:29:59.300
 So like learning more about, learning more and talking to him more about sort of what, what the doctor is saying and what that actually means, you know, looking at what are the milk grant, like, what are we really talking about in terms of how much salt you can have per day?

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:04.620
 And like, how can we, how can we creatively disperse that in food so that it still tastes good?

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:07.670
 So that's, you know, that's what it looks like with my uncle.

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:20.340
 But, and, and I think it also speaks to having multi-gener, like many generations that are sort of part of this conversation rather than targeting the individual, which I think is also an extreme problem about how we talk about behavior.

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:21.740
 Behavior never happens in a vacuum.

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:27.670
 And we, and especially in the West, we spend a lot of time talking about you as an individual as responsible for your behavior.

00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:39.370
 And the reality is that, you know, if you're not cooking the meals or if you are cooking the meals, but there are six other people in your house or, you know, you have to be able to be flexible to all of those things, right?

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:41.250
 You're, you're, you're, you're, you're feeding a community.

00:30:41.990 --> 00:31:05.700
 So with my uncle, you know, the, the, the, the, I think the, one of the reasons why so far he's been very successful, you know, with, with this, this new, this new world of like no salt or low salt is in part because he has several generations of people with different experiences that are all sort of helping or exploring this sort of new terrain with him.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:10.530
 And I also think he made a choice, you know, he made a choice and he was allowed to make that choice.

00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:12.330
 It's not that this hasn't been a conversation.

00:31:12.450 --> 00:31:16.890
 This hasn't been, his choice hasn't been controlled by people around him.

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:21.240
 I think he, he really got to make a choice about how he wanted to live and he's chosen.

00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:22.780
 Yeah.

00:31:23.020 --> 00:31:28.430
 And so in that town, what does, what does that look like for other people?

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:35.480
 I think this really challenges that myth of deficiency also to, to just hear the story of your uncle.

00:31:35.480 --> 00:31:37.750
 Like he could have also made the choice.

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:41.380
 Like, nope, I love salt and this is it.

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:42.120
 Like I'm 90.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:43.540
 Like, yeah, it did it.

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:44.620
 Like, this is amazing.

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:45.140
 We're good.

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:45.480
 Yeah.

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:45.740
 Yeah.

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:47.280
 You know, I'm sort of been okay.

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:48.000
 Yeah, exactly.

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:48.420
 Exactly.

00:31:48.460 --> 00:31:49.360
 Like I'm good with this.

00:31:49.640 --> 00:31:49.800
 Yeah.

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:50.000
 Yeah.

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:50.520
 He could have.

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:50.660
 Right.

00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:51.480
 And that'd have to be respected.

00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:52.880
 I don't think it would have been respected.

00:31:53.120 --> 00:31:57.520
 You know, like I think people would have been, you know, advocating that he do something else

00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:00.940
 or people would have, you know, or someone, someone in the family would have hired a health

00:32:00.940 --> 00:32:03.220
 care worker that would have made all the meals, you know, or whatever.

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:04.340
 This is what happens.

00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:09.340
 I think that's the thing that I'm most interested in about this project I'm working on actually

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:15.480
 is about, is about how do you create models where this, where, where we can start to play

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:22.200
 with this idea about how do you inform, how do you inform behavior when it's not necessarily

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:24.000
 embedded in a specific family?

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:31.160
 So how do you sort of, um, accumulate or collect, um, and my, you know, I'm positing

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:36.500
 it through stories and maybe through like this webisode idea, but how do you collect community

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:40.200
 information and knowledge, um, among specifically elders?

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:48.610
 Um, and how do you transfer, how do you, uh, transmit that information to others in the community?

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:54.350
 Um, cause I don't actually know, I don't know what it looks like when you don't have, um,

00:32:54.390 --> 00:32:58.150
 your family or you don't, you're not embedded in a group of people where they're a multi-generational

00:32:58.150 --> 00:33:00.490
 group of people that can help to support your, your choices.

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:00.810
 Right.

00:33:00.830 --> 00:33:03.710
 I mean, I, I actually, I mean, I do know what it looks like when we think about it as sort

00:33:03.710 --> 00:33:08.890
 of failure, but I'm not sure what it looks like from a success sort of, you know, mindset.

00:33:08.890 --> 00:33:14.780
 Um, and I want to approach it from a success mindset, not, you know, if you were a, if you're

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:21.790
 a, if you're like an elderly woman and you're living alone and you're in your seventies, um,

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:26.850
 and you don't have, you know, in the community I'm working with in working in right now, there

00:33:26.850 --> 00:33:28.610
 are always people coming in and out of the house.

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:34.180
 But if those people are not, um, either are, are not by choice or don't have the luxury

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:39.040
 or don't have the time to really engage with you around food choices, how is it that other

00:33:39.040 --> 00:33:41.260
 people, other young people in the community could engage with you?

00:33:41.300 --> 00:33:42.300
 Like what would be the avenue?

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:47.270
 How would you create a structure or a system where these people of different age groups

00:33:47.270 --> 00:33:50.410
 could talk to one another and share this information and share this knowledge?

00:33:50.670 --> 00:33:53.840
 So that's what I'm curious about, you know, would that be through stories?

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:55.400
 Do this, does that have to happen in person?

00:33:55.500 --> 00:33:56.940
 Could it happen through technology?

00:33:57.220 --> 00:33:58.880
 Would it be a mentoring relationship?

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:01.540
 Would it be a, would it be like a cooking clinic?

00:34:01.820 --> 00:34:03.560
 You know, what would that look like?

00:34:03.880 --> 00:34:06.440
 Um, but that's, yeah, that's actually what I'm interested in.

00:34:06.970 --> 00:34:07.110
 Yeah.

00:34:07.350 --> 00:34:08.490
 And so what do you think?

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:11.150
 I mean, what could it be?

00:34:11.250 --> 00:34:11.410
 Yeah.

00:34:11.410 --> 00:34:13.290
 That was a whole list of like 80 things.

00:34:13.370 --> 00:34:14.050
 Well, I don't know.

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:14.650
 I mean, I don't know.

00:34:14.690 --> 00:34:15.430
 I mean, that's the thing, right?

00:34:15.430 --> 00:34:17.530
 The next step for this is the next step is okay.

00:34:17.530 --> 00:34:20.970
 I've had these stories and I've been completely inspired and amazed and I've met these incredible

00:34:20.970 --> 00:34:25.950
 people and I go to this homecoming thing every year and we lay out all the family foods and

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:28.770
 it's, I'm spending a lot of time with these old people and I'm excited.

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:34.740
 And then I'm very much aware of the fact that, um, you know, in the Delta and in this

00:34:34.740 --> 00:34:38.520
 County, um, the outcomes for kids in particular are horrendous.

00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:43.620
 Like, you know, the obesity outcomes for kids, um, under five, I mean, it's, it's, it's

00:34:43.620 --> 00:34:45.180
 really, it's really, really bad.

00:34:45.180 --> 00:34:46.240
 Like it's endemic, right?

00:34:46.900 --> 00:34:50.950
 So then I start to think there's this gap, right?

00:34:50.950 --> 00:34:51.830
 There's this huge gap.

00:34:51.930 --> 00:34:55.250
 I'm talking to people that are in their eighties and nineties that are telling me, do you know

00:34:55.250 --> 00:34:56.270
 how to make a mincemeat pie?

