00:49:50.330 --> 00:49:59.910
 And I think, um, I think when that, when the, right now the, the, the paradigm is older people

00:49:59.910 --> 00:50:03.890
 with this knowledge that we accept its knowledge and we accept as being valuable because they're

00:50:03.890 --> 00:50:08.890
 old and they've had this experience and younger people that are educated, you know, right.

00:50:09.010 --> 00:50:14.420
 Um, I think when we're able to turn that to sort of, you know, turn the Rubik's cube a little

00:50:14.420 --> 00:50:14.880
 bit, right.

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:19.420
 And accept sort of various types of education and knowledge that come from at different ages

00:50:19.420 --> 00:50:21.080
 for different reasons, from different experiences.

00:50:21.080 --> 00:50:24.360
 I think then, then we'll see what you're talking about, right.

00:50:24.360 --> 00:50:31.000
 Then we'll see sort of these, um, a diversity of ways that stories are collected and educate

00:50:31.000 --> 00:50:35.100
 and information is collected and absorbed and sort of, you know, that's when we'll see the

00:50:35.100 --> 00:50:35.440
 diversity.

00:50:35.440 --> 00:50:39.390
 I think right now it's, um, you know, even, even myself, right.

00:50:39.390 --> 00:50:42.230
 I'm not, I'm not really going to hang out with people that are my age.

00:50:42.230 --> 00:50:44.970
 I'm going to hang out with people in their nineties because I find it fascinating because

00:50:44.970 --> 00:50:51.960
 I come from, you know, a peer group that, you know, I have, I spend enough time with people

00:50:51.960 --> 00:50:53.080
 like myself basically.

00:50:53.600 --> 00:50:53.960
 Right.

00:50:53.960 --> 00:51:00.980
 So, so when I'm working, I'm particularly interested in, in people that I wouldn't, you know, that

00:51:00.980 --> 00:51:03.750
 I, that I'm not hearing stories from sort of on a daily basis.

00:51:05.300 --> 00:51:12.330
 And what do you think in terms of, in terms of the listeners, like if you feel sort of

00:51:12.330 --> 00:51:16.090
 interested in what this would look like in your own life, how would you do that?

00:51:16.210 --> 00:51:17.710
 Like, how do you, that's something.

00:51:17.830 --> 00:51:21.970
 So that was something that early, early days when I was first starting to ask questions,

00:51:21.970 --> 00:51:25.860
 I was thinking about how amazing would it be if there was a template for this?

00:51:25.860 --> 00:51:29.580
 Like how amazing would it be if this was part of, you know, first for me, it was about

00:51:29.580 --> 00:51:30.900
 learning about where my family came from.

00:51:30.980 --> 00:51:35.220
 This was not about, you know, and, and, and like I said, food was an amazing way to start

00:51:35.220 --> 00:51:37.580
 a conversation with people that really wouldn't talk to me about anything else.

00:51:37.940 --> 00:51:41.820
 So we'd start to talk about food or we start to make something, which I think is also part

00:51:41.820 --> 00:51:42.160
 of it.

00:51:42.380 --> 00:51:43.220
 We'd make something.

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:47.520
 And while we were talking about what we were making, they would tell me stories about the

00:51:47.520 --> 00:51:49.920
 history of this, either the food or their own history.

00:51:50.020 --> 00:51:51.640
 And education was a huge part of that.

00:51:51.780 --> 00:51:57.510
 Talking about which foods went together and these, these really crazy tests that kids used

00:51:57.510 --> 00:52:02.090
 to take in Mississippi, where they would say, um, it was about, uh, what's, what's that thing

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:07.080
 where you have to like match, um, like the, the, like the spoon with the kind of food or

00:52:07.080 --> 00:52:09.960
 like a fork, a fork and a spoon, like what do you use for each thing?

00:52:09.960 --> 00:52:11.780
 Like these really ridiculous tests.

00:52:12.140 --> 00:52:18.710
 Um, and one of them, or, or like cornbread goes with, um, like what do you eat cornbread

00:52:18.710 --> 00:52:18.950
 with?

00:52:18.990 --> 00:52:19.190
 Right.

00:52:19.210 --> 00:52:23.290
 And there were these assumptions and it was supposed to be like this intelligence test, but with

00:52:23.290 --> 00:52:27.310
 kids, with black kids in particular and black Southern kids, there are very, very specific

00:52:27.310 --> 00:52:30.230
 ideas about which foods are eaten with which thing and at which time.

00:52:30.230 --> 00:52:31.870
 And so they were failing these tests.

00:52:32.290 --> 00:52:35.850
 Um, and it was a disaster and they were saying that the kids were stupid and like all this

00:52:35.850 --> 00:52:35.990
 stuff.

00:52:36.070 --> 00:52:40.790
 And so I have all of these recordings of these older people in their seventies talking about

00:52:40.790 --> 00:52:45.750
 how, how bizarre, you know, she's now in education, like if she went on to do a master's

00:52:45.750 --> 00:52:47.550
 and things like that, and then now lives back in Mississippi.

00:52:47.750 --> 00:52:52.330
 And she was talking about how bizarre it was to now reflect on those things and realize

00:52:52.330 --> 00:52:57.210
 that outside of the South, there's a completely different way of like understanding how food,

00:52:57.270 --> 00:52:58.850
 like which are vegetables, things like that.

00:52:58.850 --> 00:53:00.230
 Like, what do you consider to be a vegetable?

00:53:01.040 --> 00:53:05.440
 Totally different answers, you know, like our collards, a vegetable or green beans, a vegetable

00:53:05.440 --> 00:53:06.400
 and that those are actually different.

00:53:06.400 --> 00:53:08.060
 Like, you know, they're cause it's like a cabbage.

00:53:08.060 --> 00:53:08.840
 So that's different.

00:53:08.840 --> 00:53:13.840
 Like, so, you know, just a lot of, a lot of, um, that, which is completely, completely,

00:53:13.840 --> 00:53:14.620
 completely off track.

00:53:14.620 --> 00:53:16.440
 And I can't remember the question, but anyway, yeah.

00:53:16.930 --> 00:53:20.200
 The question was, how would I do this?

00:53:20.410 --> 00:53:21.050
 Oh, right.

00:53:21.170 --> 00:53:25.670
 So I think, I think it would be, and this is long, you know, like, I think this is where

00:53:25.670 --> 00:53:29.510
 you, you test approaches and then you see for yourself that if you can actually

00:53:29.510 --> 00:53:33.790
 do it not five times, not 10, but 50 times, you know, and if it really works, right.

00:53:33.790 --> 00:53:37.870
 And then how are these useful to people, you know, and then how do you share these stories?

00:53:38.270 --> 00:53:39.550
 I'm so far from that.

00:53:39.590 --> 00:53:43.010
 Like, so, you know, I'm, I'm, I'm at the point where I'm thinking, let's have a dinner

00:53:43.010 --> 00:53:47.990
 and, um, and make some food and project the sound of people telling you what the recipe

00:53:47.990 --> 00:53:50.250
 or cook the recipe live, you know, with a dinner.

00:53:50.410 --> 00:53:52.070
 I don't know if that's sort of where I'm at, right.

00:53:52.530 --> 00:54:00.400
 I think it would be really, really exciting to think of this idea of people that are like

00:54:00.400 --> 00:54:06.060
 a story collection template around food and being someone who, um, is interested in a part

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:10.920
 of your family history and to create an approach that was sort of replicable in some ways for

00:54:10.920 --> 00:54:14.620
 how you could go into your family history or how you could go to the town where your family

00:54:14.620 --> 00:54:21.000
 comes from and investigate and explore your history through asking these questions about

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:26.460
 food to give people sort of a place to start from, you know, and, you know, when you kind

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:32.470
 of blow that out as even if it were 10 people that decided to do this, what would it be like

00:54:32.470 --> 00:54:36.470
 to have just a completely different way of viewing the history of certain locations in the United

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:41.880
 States or around the world that was completely informed by people's, you know, both familial

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:47.340
 sort of line and culinary history, but people's interviews and stories with, with these, you

00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:49.070
 know, these ambassadors, basically.

00:54:51.330 --> 00:54:51.450
 Yeah.

00:54:51.650 --> 00:54:52.890
 Well, let's, let's end it there.

00:54:52.990 --> 00:54:53.550
 That's great.

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:53.970
 Okay.

00:54:54.170 --> 00:54:54.550
 Yeah.

00:54:54.630 --> 00:54:55.010
 Perfect.

00:54:55.190 --> 00:54:55.370
 Great.

